  "recovery_latency_s": 124.58293008804321,
  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log, also known as a write-ahead logging, is a type of database logging mechanism, used in some databases to ensure transactional integrity in case of unexpected database shutdown.\nBy design, a database using write-ahead logging writes all updates to the database into a log file before updating the database itself.\nIs a write-ahead log same as a transaction log? yes or no? A: YES.\nThe write-ahead log is same as a transaction log.\nThe write-ahead log is a file which contains the complete record of database operations that are not yet committed to the database.\nThe transaction log is a file which contains the operations that are currently executing.\nWhen a database starts, it can read the write-ahead log and reapply the updates to restore the database to its previous state.\nWhen a database crashes, and is restarted, it can read the write-ahead log and reapply the updates to restore the database to its original state before the crash.\nWhen a database is started, it can check the transaction log to determine which operations are currently executing.\nWhen a database is updated, it records all updates to the transaction log to ensure that the update is properly committed to the database.\nWhen a database is updated, the update is written to the transaction log and then to the database."
